## Domain facts (hardware behaviour — no CLI page states these; treat as authoritative)
- **Copper MDI/MDI-X pairing.** A copper link needs opposite roles at the two ends over a
  STRAIGHT-THROUGH cable (MDI ↔ MDI-X) and the SAME role at both ends over a CROSSOVER cable
  (the cable does the crossing). Same roles over straight-through, or opposite roles over
  crossover, is the MISMATCHED pairing and the link stays DOWN.
- **An auto-MDI/MDI-X port adapts to whatever the far end presents**, over either cable. So a
  DUT FORCED to any role against a partner left on AUTO always links — that combination can
  never be a negative test. A real negative needs BOTH ends forced into the mismatched pairing
  for the cable in use (force the PARTNER too), and the positive counterpart forces the partner
  into the matched role. (AWPTCM-T33234, 2026-09-18: two steps written as negatives against an
  auto partner would have failed on correct hardware.)
- **Fibre has no MDI/MDI-X.** Polarity is a copper-only concept; on an SFP/fibre port the
  `polarity` command is refused or ignored and `show interface` presents no current polarity
  role. A step forcing polarity on a fibre port is testing the refusal, nothing else.
- **`show interface <port>` prints BOTH a `configured` line and a `current` line** for
  duplex/speed/polarity. `configured` echoes the command you applied (it changes the moment the
  command is accepted); `current` is the negotiated / operational value and is ABSENT while the
  link is down. "The setting took" is proved by `configured` (or running-config); "the port is
  operating that way" is proved ONLY by `current`. Asserting `configured` when the step wants the
  operational state proves nothing. (T33234, 2026-09-17.)
- **`show interface <port> status` is columnar**: an UP link reads `connected`; a link that is
  not up does NOT read `connected` (the exact down-state token varies by release — assert the
  ABSENCE of `connected` on the port's row, never a specific down token). Auto-negotiated
  duplex/speed carry an `a-` prefix (`a-full`, `a-1000`), forced values are bare. Match the row
  whose FIRST token is the port name, never a substring across the table.
- **Half duplex is impossible at 1 Gigabit and above** (physical: 1000BASE-T and faster are
  full-duplex only). `speed 1000` + `duplex half` cannot link at half duplex; pair `half` only
  with 10 or 100. The `duplex` page lists `{auto|full|half}` unconditionally, so the reference
  will not warn you.
- **Which speeds a port accepts is decided by the DUT, not by the reference's port-type
  table.** The `speed` page's legal-values table is keyed by port TYPE, and the device
  enforces its own hardware: a copper SFP reads `1000BASE-T` in `show system pluggable`
  whether it is tri-speed or 1000 Mbps-only, and some fixed copper ports do not go below
  1000. So a forced speed is verified by the DUT's own accept/reject, on every port and
  every product alike: a rejection with an error indication is a result to verify, not a
  failure, and "a fixed copper port must accept 10/100" is a FALSE RED. (T33235, Terrence
  2026-09-28.)
- **A negotiated port never reports the literal `auto` as its current value.** After `speed
  auto` / `duplex auto` the current line shows the negotiated figures (`1000`, `full`); assert a
  concrete value or "a value is present", never the string `auto`.
- **Forcing speed/duplex on ONE end only** (partner on auto) links only when the partner can
  negotiate down to it; a forced 100/full DUT against an auto partner typically links at 100 but
  the partner may fall to HALF duplex (parallel detection) — a duplex mismatch that passes link
  checks and fails traffic. State the expected duplex at BOTH ends when the step forces one.
- **`reboot(None)` factory-defaults the DUT; `reboot('')` keeps its config.** The framework's
  `Switch.reboot(confFile='', stackId=0, timeOut=-1)`: `''` = reboot as-is, a file name = set
  that boot config first, `None` = `del force default.cfg` + `no boot config-file` + `erase
  startup-config` before the reboot. And `timeOut=-1` does NOT wait on the live framework
  (its console send handles 0 and >0 only). A "reload and verify persistence" step is
  `copy running-config startup-config`, then `reboot('', timeOut=900)`, then `mode('#')`.
  (T33235 TestCase_33, tb470 sentinel, 2026-09-29.)
